---
deadlock_detection: false
---

# live_transcript_sync: how an open thread in the web UI stays equal to
# the session's transcript on disk (go/tests/model/README.md).
#
# Server (go/internal/serve): the supervisor turns child output into
# events. A recorded kind takes the next seq, enters the replay ring (500
# in the product) and fans out to every subscriber (supervisor.go
# emitLocked, fanoutLocked). A streaming delta is buffered 50ms, never
# recorded, and fans out with Seq 0 (deltas.go); a recorded event flushes
# the buffer first; delta-reset is live only. A subscriber whose channel
# is full (256 in the product, CAP here) is closed and removed, never
# waited for. GET /api/sessions/{id}/events subscribes BEFORE it replays
# the ring, writes Seq-0 frames with no SSE id line (api.go events,
# writeEvent) and ignores Last-Event-ID: every EventSource reconnect
# replays the whole ring.
#
# Page (web/src/app.tsx, the `selected` effect): selecting a session
# clears the lines, resets the cursor, GETs the whole transcript and
# subscribes. An event is a change signal, not a line: a recorded one
# arms a 120ms catch-up, GET ?since=<last line's seq>, whose entries are
# appended deduped by history seq. A failed catch-up pauses the thread
# ("Updates paused") and re-arms on a 4s..30s backoff; Retry forces one.
# Responses for a session no longer selected are ignored (`live`).
#
# The mapped states are field values: Unsubscribed sel == -1; Live conn
# "live"; SubscriberDropped conn "dropped" with frames still in the
# channel; Reconnecting conn "dropped" with none; CatchingUp fetch.sess
# >= 0; Paused paused; Switched loaded False after a Switch.
#
# No role field is ever None: fizzbee-mbt 0.2.0 has no None on the wire
# (an adapter can send nothing that compares equal to it), so "none" is
# spelled sess = -1 for the two request slots and id = 0 for a frame
# without an SSE id line.
#
# Abstractions, each to keep the graph small:
# - one live session (#0) whose child records MAXREC entries; session #1
#   is a finished session with STATIC_DISK entries, there to switch to.
# - MAXREC = RING = 1: one entry is enough for every race below, and
#   ring eviction changes none of the claims (a reconnect's catch-up
#   reads the file, not the ring).
# - a recorded event and a transcript entry are one-to-one, so a ring
#   frame carries the history seq it announces. In the product the two
#   numberings differ (supervisor seq vs history seq) and the page never
#   mixes them; neither does this model.
# - the 50ms delta buffer is one flushed frame (Ephemeral), and a
#   delta-reset is the same frame (Seq 0, live only); what the page draws
#   from them is a preview the next record replaces, so it is not state
#   here. The flush-before-record order only matters to that preview.
# - a stream that ends for any other reason (an idle proxy the ": ping"
#   heartbeat failed to keep open, the network) is, to the page, the same
#   as the server dropping a stalled subscriber: EventSource retries and
#   the ring comes again. Only the channel-full drop is modelled.
# - one catch-up in flight: in the product a second timer can fire while
#   one is pending; both append deduped. Backoff timing is not modelled:
#   a failed catch-up just re-arms.
# - one initial GET slot: Switch replaces it, which is what the page's
#   `live` guard makes of the old one. Its read (InitialServe) is a step
#   apart from its landing (InitialOk) because the page REPLACES its
#   lines with that read.
#
# Fairness: the page's own steps (responses landing, timers firing, the
# EventSource retrying, reading its stream) are strongly fair; the person
# (Open, Switch, Retry), the child (Ephemeral, Record) and the network
# (CatchUpFail) are not. Strong, not weak: a person switching forever
# disables each page step again and again, and weak fairness would let
# that starve it. A catch-up that fails every time it is asked is the one
# outage the liveness claims do not survive.
#
# Corrections to the mapped model, from the code:
# - "Subscribed(replaying ring)" -> "Live" is one step (replay()): the
#   handler writes the ring before it reads its channel, and a frame
#   fanned out in between is written twice — duplicates the page folds
#   into one catch-up, never a loss. A separate replay state only added
#   interleavings nothing observes.
# - "SubscriberDropped" keeps the frames already in the channel: the
#   handler reads a closed Go channel dry before it ends the response.
# - a replayed `call` start whose end was evicted cannot happen by
#   eviction: the ring drops oldest first, so an end never leaves while
#   its start stays.
#
# Where the product does not hold, ConvergesToDisk says so (see its
# comment): an initial GET that lands after a catch-up throws the
# catch-up's lines away.

MAXREC = 1      # entries the live session records
RING = 1        # replay ring capacity (500 in the product)
CAP = 1         # subscriber channel buffer (256 in the product)
STATIC_DISK = 1 # entries of the finished session #1

# Helpers are plain functions: a fizz func called from an atomic action
# still yields, and fizz would interleave other actions inside it.

def frame(kind, seq):
    # writeEvent: an SSE id line only for a recorded seq (0 = no line).
    id = 0
    if seq > 0:
        id = seq
    return record(kind=kind, seq=seq, id=id)

def fanout(sel, conn, inbox, f):
    # fanoutLocked, to session #0's subscriber: returns (conn, inbox).
    if sel == 0 and conn == "live":
        if len(inbox) < CAP:
            return (conn, inbox + [f])
        # A full channel is closed and removed; the scanner goes on.
        return ("dropped", inbox)
    return (conn, inbox)

def armed(timer, f):
    # Only a recorded kind is something to catch up on.
    return timer or f.kind == "rec"

def replay(sel, ring, timer):
    # GET .../events: subscribe, then write the whole ring (Last-Event-ID
    # is ignored). Returns the page's catch-up timer after it.
    if sel == 1:
        ring = [frame("rec", STATIC_DISK)]
    for f in ring:
        timer = armed(timer, f)
    return timer

def disk_of(sess, disk):
    if sess == 0:
        return disk
    return STATIC_DISK

def last_seq(lines):
    # The cursor: the last line's seq, 0 with none.
    n = 0
    for l in lines:
        n = l.seq
    return n

role Sync:
    action Init:
        self.disk = 0         # entries on disk, session #0
        self.ring = []        # replay ring of session #0 (frames)
        self.sel = -1         # selected session; -1 = none (Unsubscribed)
        self.conn = "none"    # none | live | dropped
        self.inbox = []       # the subscriber's channel: frames not yet read
        self.lines = []       # the page's lines: record(sess, seq)
        self.loaded = False   # the first GET for sel landed
        # initial GET in flight: record(sess, snap); sess -1 = none, snap -1 = not read yet
        self.get = record(sess=-1, snap=-1)
        # catch-up in flight: record(sess, since); sess -1 = none
        self.fetch = record(sess=-1, since=0)
        self.timer = False    # catch-up armed
        self.paused = False   # "Updates paused"

    # --- server: session #0's child ---

    atomic action Ephemeral:
        # A flushed delta run (or a delta-reset): Seq 0, fanned out live,
        # never recorded.
        require self.disk < MAXREC
        f = frame("delta", 0)
        (self.conn, self.inbox) = fanout(self.sel, self.conn, self.inbox, f)

    atomic action Record:
        require self.disk < MAXREC
        self.disk = self.disk + 1
        f = frame("rec", self.disk)
        self.ring = (self.ring + [f])[-RING:]
        (self.conn, self.inbox) = fanout(self.sel, self.conn, self.inbox, f)

    # --- the page ---

    atomic action Open:
        require self.sel == -1
        self.sel = 0
        self.conn = "live"
        self.timer = replay(self.sel, self.ring, self.timer)
        self.get = record(sess=0, snap=-1)

    atomic action Switch:
        require self.sel >= 0
        # The effect's cleanup closes the EventSource (the server drops
        # the subscription and its channel); the new run clears the
        # lines, the cursor and the pause, GETs and subscribes.
        self.sel = 1 - self.sel
        self.lines = []
        self.loaded = False
        self.timer = False
        self.paused = False
        self.conn = "live"
        self.inbox = []
        self.timer = replay(self.sel, self.ring, self.timer)
        self.get = record(sess=self.sel, snap=-1)
        lost = False

    atomic fair<strong> action InitialServe:
        require self.get.sess >= 0 and self.get.snap == -1
        n = disk_of(self.get.sess, self.disk)
        self.get = record(sess=self.get.sess, snap=n)

    atomic fair<strong> action InitialOk:
        require self.get.sess >= 0 and self.get.snap >= 0
        g = self.get
        self.get = record(sess=-1, snap=-1)
        # setLines(r.entries): a replace, not a merge. A catch-up that
        # landed first may have added lines this older read lacks.
        if len([l for l in self.lines if l.seq > g.snap]) > 0:
            lost = True
        self.lines = [record(sess=g.sess, seq=s) for s in range(1, g.snap + 1)]
        self.loaded = True

    atomic fair<strong> action Deliver:
        require len(self.inbox) > 0
        f = self.inbox[0]
        self.inbox = self.inbox[1:]
        self.timer = armed(self.timer, f)

    atomic fair<strong> action Reconnect:
        # EventSource retries once the stream ended. Last-Event-ID is
        # ignored, so the whole ring comes again.
        require self.conn == "dropped" and len(self.inbox) == 0
        self.conn = "live"
        self.timer = replay(self.sel, self.ring, self.timer)

    atomic fair<strong> action CatchUpFire:
        require self.timer and self.fetch.sess < 0
        self.timer = False
        self.fetch = record(sess=self.sel, since=last_seq(self.lines))

    atomic action Retry:
        require self.paused and self.fetch.sess < 0
        self.timer = False
        self.fetch = record(sess=self.sel, since=last_seq(self.lines))

    atomic fair<strong> action CatchUpOk:
        require self.fetch.sess >= 0
        f = self.fetch
        self.fetch = record(sess=-1, since=0)
        if f.sess == self.sel:
            self.paused = False
            n = disk_of(f.sess, self.disk)
            have = [l.seq for l in self.lines]
            add = [record(sess=f.sess, seq=s) for s in range(f.since + 1, n + 1) if s not in have]
            self.lines = self.lines + add
            if len(add) > 0:
                # Healed. A catch-up asked before the clobber, from the
                # cursor it moved back, brings nothing and heals nothing.
                lost = False

    atomic action CatchUpFail:
        require self.fetch.sess >= 0
        f = self.fetch
        self.fetch = record(sess=-1, since=0)
        if f.sess == self.sel:
            self.paused = True
            self.timer = True

action Init:
    sync = Sync()
    # Ghost (not product state, not compared by MBT): the page's first
    # read replaced lines a catch-up had already added, and no catch-up
    # has added them back since. See ConvergesToDisk.
    lost = False

# Seq-0 frames never carry an SSE id line: a reconnecting EventSource
# must never resume from text that was never recorded.
always assertion EphemeralFramesHaveNoId:
    for f in sync.inbox + sync.ring:
        if f.seq == 0 and f.id != 0:
            return False
    return True

# Seq-0 frames never enter the ring, so a late joiner never replays a
# delta-reset (or a delta) for text it never saw.
always assertion RingHoldsRecordsOnly:
    for f in sync.ring:
        if f.seq == 0 or f.kind != "rec":
            return False
    return True

# Transcript entries are unique by history seq, whatever was replayed.
always assertion LinesUniqueBySeq:
    seen = []
    for l in sync.lines:
        if l.seq in seen:
            return False
        seen.append(l.seq)
    return True

# A stalled subscriber never blocks the stdout scanner: fan-out never
# queues past the channel's buffer (it drops the subscriber instead).
always assertion ScannerNeverBlocked:
    return len(sync.inbox) <= CAP

# Switching never shows the previous session's lines under the new
# header: until the first read lands the thread shows none.
always assertion NoForeignLines:
    if not sync.loaded:
        return True
    for l in sync.lines:
        if l.sess != sync.sel:
            return False
    return True

# A dropped or reconnected subscriber converges to the transcript on disk.
#
# Except when `lost`, a product gap this model found. Open (or Switch)
# GETs the whole transcript and subscribes at once. If the GET's read is
# taken before an entry is recorded, and its response lands after the
# catch-up that entry's event triggered, setLines replaces the caught-up
# lines with the older read and the cursor goes back with them. Nothing
# arms another catch-up until the next recorded event, so after a turn's
# last entry the thread stays one entry short with nothing saying so.
# Without `or lost` fizz fails this; the shortest such run is Open,
# InitialServe, Record, Deliver, CatchUpFire, CatchUpOk, InitialOk, after
# which only the person or the child can move anything.
# The mapped claim "converges after the next recorded event" holds: a
# later entry's catch-up adds the missing one back and clears `lost`.
always eventually assertion ConvergesToDisk:
    if sync.sel == -1 or lost:
        return True
    n = STATIC_DISK
    if sync.sel == 0:
        n = sync.disk
    return sync.loaded and [l.seq for l in sync.lines] == list(range(1, n + 1))

# "Updates paused" clears after the next successful catch-up.
always eventually assertion PausedClears:
    return not sync.paused
